                      "body":  "## Executive summary\n\nIntroduces a Git-first, zero-runtime-dependency internationalization architecture for English, Spanish and Brazilian Portuguese across the Astro + React application.\n\nThis PR replaces locale-suffixed route IDs such as `/qa.pt-br` with canonical locale URLs (`/pt-br/qa`), localizes the global UX shell, adds locale-aware content resolution, and establishes CI quality gates for catalogs, Markdown integrity, production builds and Playwright E2E.\n\n## Architecture\n\n- **Canonical URLs**\n  - English: `/`, `/frontend`\n  - Spanish: `/es`, `/es/frontend`\n  - Brazilian Portuguese: `/pt-br`, `/pt-br/frontend`\n- **Middleware rewrite without a second render phase**\n- **Permanent migration redirects** from legacy `*.es`, `*.pt-br` and `*.en` route IDs\n- **English fallback** when localized metadata or topic Markdown is unavailable\n- **No additional production package** and no lockfile change\n- **Native `Intl` formatters** for date, number and explicit-currency formatting\n- **Shared server/client catalogs** for Astro and React islands\n\n## UX/UI coverage delivered\n\n- Home page SEO and headings\n- Hero\n- Desktop/mobile navigation\n- Language switcher preserving the current route\n- Featured status labels\n- Footer and accessibility labels\n- Open-source/community banner\n- Changelog banner and locale-aware dates\n- Roadmap header, tabs, related resources and internal links\n- Dynamic roadmap metadata selection\n- Localized topic Markdown resolution\n\n## SEO and accessibility\n\n- Dynamic `\u003chtml lang\u003e` and `Content-Language`\n- Locale-aware canonical URLs\n- `hreflang` for `en`, `es`, `pt-BR` and `x-default`\n- Open Graph locale and alternates\n- No-JavaScript language switcher\n- Localized navigation and social-link accessibility labels\n\n## Content conventions\n\n```text\nfrontend.md\nfrontend.es.md\nfrontend.pt-br.md\n\ncontent/topic@id.md\ncontent/topic@id.es.md\ncontent/topic@id.pt-br.md\n```\n\nLocalized files are variants of one canonical resource ID and no longer become independent routes.\n\n## Security and performance\n\n- Removes an unused roadmap metadata read from every topic request\n- Validates resolved topic paths and prevents traversal outside the roadmap content directory\n- Fixes the duplicate `vite` configuration that previously caused the first block to be overwritten\n- Keeps localization server-rendered and hydration-free\n- Uses in-place Astro middleware rewrites\n\n## Quality gates\n\nNew commands:\n\n```bash\npnpm i18n:check\npnpm i18n:content\npnpm i18n:coverage\npnpm i18n:validate\npnpm test:e2e:i18n\n```\n\nThe `i18n Quality Gate` workflow validates:\n\n1. Catalog key parity\n2. Placeholder parity\n3. Localized Markdown source pairing\n4. Code-fence integrity\n5. Translation coverage artifact\n6. Production build\n7. Chromium locale E2E\n\n## OSS toolchain\n\nThe runtime requires only Astro and native platform APIs. The documented optional workflow supports:\n\n- Languine CLI for developer automation\n- Weblate or self-hosted Tolgee for TMS/review\n- LibreTranslate for self-hosted machine-translation suggestions\n- inlang Sherlock for editor integration\n- Playwright and axe-core for QA\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe architecture and critical global UX shell are implemented in all three locales. Existing Portuguese Markdown is automatically consumed under canonical PT-BR routes. Spanish and any missing PT-BR documents safely fall back to English and are measured by the generated coverage report; fallback is intentionally not counted as translated coverage.\n\nSee `docs/i18n/README.md` for the operating model and the objective criteria required before declaring any locale 100% complete.",
